Analyze the following Dapper query in C# targeting a SQL Server table where 'ProductCode' is defined as 'VARCHAR(50)' with a non-clustered index:

public async Task<Product> GetProductAsync(IDbConnection db, string code) {
    return await db.QueryFirstOrDefaultAsync<Product>(
        "SELECT Id, ProductCode, Name FROM Products WHERE ProductCode = @code",
        new { code }
    );
}

MANDATORY REQUIREMENTS:
1. Explain why Dapper maps C# 'string' types by default to 'NVARCHAR' (Unicode), forcing SQL Server to perform an implicit conversion (CONVERT_IMPLICIT) on the VARCHAR column, negating Index Seek capability.
2. Refactor the parameter in Dapper using 'DbString' with 'IsAnsi = true' (or 'AnsiString') to explicitly force VARCHAR and preserve index performance.

Analyze this C# Dapper method processing a massive log export report:

public async Task<List<LogEntry>> ExportLogsAsync(IDbConnection db, DateTime startDate) {
    var logs = await db.QueryAsync<LogEntry>("SELECT Id, Timestamp, Message, StackTrace FROM SystemLogs WHERE Timestamp >= @startDate", new { startDate });
    return logs.ToList();
}

MANDATORY REQUIREMENTS:
1. Explain why the default 'buffered: true' parameter in Dapper loads the entire result set into RAM before returning, risking an OutOfMemoryException on large tables.
2. Refactor the query passing 'buffered: false' to stream rows row-by-row through an 'IEnumerable' without buffering the entire dataset in memory.

Analyze the following C# service code using Dapper to fetch orders and items:

public async Task<List<OrderDto>> GetCustomerOrdersAsync(IDbConnection db, int customerId) {
    var orders = (await db.QueryAsync<OrderDto>("SELECT Id, OrderDate, Total FROM Orders WHERE CustomerId = @customerId", new { customerId })).ToList();

    foreach (var order in orders) {
        order.Items = (await db.QueryAsync<OrderItem>("SELECT Id, ProductName, Price FROM OrderItems WHERE OrderId = @id", new { id = order.Id })).ToList();
    }
    return orders;
}

MANDATORY REQUIREMENTS:
1. Explain the catastrophic impact on network latency and database I/O overhead caused by the N+1 query pattern in Dapper.
2. Refactor the method to execute a SINGLE database call using 'db.QueryMultipleAsync()', sequentially reading both result sets with 'multi.ReadAsync<OrderDto>()' and 'multi.ReadAsync<OrderItem>()' in one clean round-trip.

Analyze the following code in a C# microservice using Dapper:

public async Task<IEnumerable<Customer>> GetCustomersByCityAsync(IDbConnection db, string city) {
    string sql = $"SELECT Id, Name, City FROM Customers WHERE City = '{city}'";
    return await db.QueryAsync<Customer>(sql);
}

MANDATORY REQUIREMENTS:
1. Explain the two critical issues caused by direct interpolation in Dapper: SQL Injection vulnerability and Query Plan Cache Pollution in SQL Server.
2. Refactor the query using Dapper anonymous parameterization ('SELECT ... WHERE City = @City', new { City = city }) to guarantee security and execution plan reuse.

Analyze the following ASP.NET Core endpoint invoked 10,000 times per second:

public async Task<User?> GetUserByIdAsync(AppDbContext db, int userId) {
    return await db.Users
        .AsNoTracking()
        .FirstOrDefaultAsync(u => u.Id == userId);
}

MANDATORY REQUIREMENTS:
1. Explain the cost of repeated LINQ compilation: on each invocation EF Core translates the expression tree to SQL, validates the model, and generates execution plans from scratch.
2. Refactor the code using 'EF.CompileAsyncQuery' to pre-compile the query once during application startup as a static field, eliminating translation overhead on hot paths.

Analyze this LINQ query against a database with 2,000,000 records:

public async Task<List<ProductDto>> GetActiveExpensiveProductsAsync(AppDbContext context) {
    var allProducts = context.Products.ToList();

    var filtered = allProducts
        .Where(p => p.IsActive && p.Price > 1000)
        .Select(p => new ProductDto { Id = p.Id, Name = p.Name })
        .Take(10)
        .ToList();

    return filtered;
}

MANDATORY REQUIREMENTS:
1. Explain the performance disaster caused by invoking '.ToList()' prematurely: downloading 2 million complete rows from the database into C# RAM and performing filtering client-side instead of in SQL Server.
2. Refactor the query maintaining the 'IQueryable' chain so that the SQL translation executes WHERE, SELECT, and TOP(10) directly in the database, postponing asynchronous materialization to the end with 'await ...ToListAsync()'.
